==Special Moves==

===Hishou Ken===
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=andy_214a,andy_214c,andy_214ac
|input=214A/C/AC
|display=180x180px
|description3=
* A regular hadouken that disappears before going fullscreen. Has THE shortest recovery of any slow fireball in the game to compensate.
* EX version is a slower close range orb with 3 hits that's always plus, good in combos and for oki.
* All versions have a shockingly large hitbox and are very active, will often catch jumping opponents.

A fireball designed for midrange usage rather than long-range zoning, but it makes up for disappearing with its short recovery. This lets Andy both throw it out while maintaining the very real threat of an anti-air if the opponent doesn't jump on prediction, and also use it as a way to get + frames on oki or when spaced out. The slow moving nature of both versions especially helps with this application, with the A version being better for offense and B for neutral, as it goes further in the same active time.

The EX version is kind of a different beast, as it completely lacks ranged beyond the space right in front of Andy, so it is most used by far as a combo tool. Beyond that, its neutral usage is restricted to keepout, where its admittedly large, disjointed, and very active hitbox comes in handy. On offense it's best used as an oki tool on account of its active frames, giving Andy some very easy-to-use projectile setplay. While there are no true strings into 214AC, it's a frametrap from 214BD > D, and plus with no pushback, so some conditioning can make it an effective pressure reset.
}}

===Kuu Hishou Ken===
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=andy_j214a,andy_j214c,andy_j214ac
|input=j.214A/C/AC
|display=180x180px
|description3=
* All versions halt Andy's jump momentum, EX version sends him backwards after the attack.
* Meterless versions can only be done from a neutral or forward jump/hop but can be very plus.
* EX version '''can be done from a backjump or even backdash''', usually safe or plus.

Behaves similar to its grounded couterpart, but moves diagonally down and has recovery until landing. While that is a big weakness, and both meterless versions are usually unrewarding on hit, this move makes Andy almost impossible to consistently anti-air. From a neutral jump, Andy can do a failling button to catch people dashing under the fireball, while succesfully doing the fireball itself will usually let him land and pressure a distant opponent while beating anti-airs. This in conjunction with 214A/B makes some characters have to work overtime to get in against Andy, and he can punish risky approaches very hard. When spaced, both meterless version are good as reversal safe meaties, though they're hard to consistently set up.

EX again is quite different, and also shockingly ignorant. It's doable from a backjump or backdash, sends Andy further back, and with how enormous its hitbox is it will rarely ever fail to let Andy safely land while retreating. It also does good damage, can lead to combos depending on height, and cancelling it into Rev Blow is a hard knockdown on hit, safe on block, and the opponent can't even guard cancel against it, while being dirt cheap. Shares its oki applications with 214BD, however it also works as a shimmy when done from a backdash or as a TK, where it can reliably lead to combos with Super.
}}

===Zan'ei Ken===
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=andy_236a,andy_236c,andy_236ac
|input=236A/C/AC
|display=200x200px
|description3=
* Has variable startup and duration depending on distance from the opponent or brake timing.

Straight dashpunch. 236A is a safe pressure ender, albeit one that leaves Andy very close to the opponent for a throw mixup. As a combo tool it's kind of bad; it ends juggles with low damage, and leaves grounded targets far from Andy and +1, unless it's the starter and CH, where it links to light normals for mostly low damage. This makes it much more useful as a guard cancel, where it's a fast option with an easy input that will frequently counterhit, and its followup combo's low damage is less relevant. It's also very quick to whiff with good mobility, although dashing is still usually better. 236C has worse frame data and better range, but it's a rekka. It's a decent ender with good corner carry, and an okay neutral tool, but it's unsafe so its followups need to be represented on block, which themselves are unrewarding and weak.

236AC is is basically a better 236C; better on block with higher reward that can be confirmed, so it's much better in neutral. It's also a core combo extender with good base damage and easy combos from it, very frequently used in stronger combos.
}}

