* Similar to B but hits behind Andy, making it very niche on 214B.
* 214BD > C instead teleports behind the opponent, which is useful to make anti-airs whiff and for sideswitch routes.

A dedicated crossup attack, but you still need to make sure you actually go over the opponent with your meterless flips since it has no front hitbox, similar to Dong's j4C. Has very similar frame data to Flip B, so it's usually safe, this time with the added utility of letting you cross up anti-air DP attempts and give people a bad input. When timed well it can even make a successful anti-air whiff entirely, though these setups aren't airtight or rewarding unless a DP is actually baited. Naturally this is best on 214D, but even from 214B it's a good way to hit someone dashing under you to make Flip A and B whiff. Also it's not an overhead but that literally does not matter.

214BD > C is the FT2 killer. Fullscreen teleport with a quick startup; while it's not unreactable, it's hard to anti-air this consistently even despite the lengthened startup. Can cancel into j214AC if it connects, which is a combo on hit and a frametrap string on block that only loses to vertical reversals. Very strong option for neutral, and on offense it's an on-demand DP bait when timed right. Like the other followups, if timed late enough it won't come out, but the teleport will actually still happen, which can catch people off guard.
